==English==
{{wikipedia|Said the actress to the bishop}}

===Phrase===
{{en-phrase}}

# {{lb|en|humorous}} used to [[highlight]] a [[sexual]] [[ambiguity]] in an [[innocent]] [[remark]]
#: ''Heavens, that's a big one – '''as the actress said to the bishop'''.''
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|1976|title=The Left in Britain|author=David Widgery
|passage=The important thing was where you do it, not what you do ('''as the actress said to the bishop''')}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|1998|The Entertainers: portraits of stardom in the twentieth century|author=Timothy White
|passage="Oh, well," offered Andrews wryly, as she peeled a piece of fruit, "nothing like a hot banana — '''as the actress said to the bishop'''." She then apologized for the tacky sexual quip, explaining, "It just slipped out — '''as the actress said to the bishop'''."}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|1999|title=Ghostly demarcations: a symposium on Jacques Derrida's Spectres of Marx|author=Michael Sprinker
|passage=He has, '''as the actress said to the bishop''', been an unconscionably long time coming,{{...}}}}

